\section{Separate Discovering Survivors From Rebuilding Free Space}

A stop-the-world mark-sweep collector does not move surviving
objects. The mark pass identifies the closure of roots under
strong reference fields; the sweep pass turns everything else
into reusable storage. Keeping the phases separate makes it
possible to debug the tracer without guessing whether a free-list
operation destroyed evidence. Aho and colleagues describe a
heap with embedded free lists as well as mark-sweep variants
\cite{aho2007compilers}. In the simplest walkable representation,
every chunk, used or free, has an extent. Store both allocation
state and the current mark state; a free chunk is not a large
object whose descriptor happens to describe no pointers.

Mark when enqueuing, not when removing from the stack. For
example, a vector may contain a thousand aliases for one child.
The first edge marks it and enqueues it; the other edges do
not enlarge the pending work. Marking must ignore integers and
other non-reference words as directed by the descriptors. It
also must not discover new roots by treating free-list links
as object fields. The work stack belongs to the collector,
which cannot casually allocate it from the heap that exhausted
its allocation budget. Use reserve memory or a defined overflow
strategy. Discarding a failed push and continuing to sweep can
reclaim an unvisited child of a marked parent.

