% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Accumulate Maximal Runs in Address Order}

\begin{verbatim}
discover(p):
    if p is null: return
    assert p names an allocated object start
    if marked(p): return
    mark(p)
    push_reserved_work_stack(p)

collect(region):
    for root in roots: discover(root.load())
    while work_stack not empty:
        for slot in strong_slots(pop_work_stack()):
            discover(slot.load())
    clear_bins_for(region)
    cursor := region.begin
    run := empty
    while cursor < region.end:
        chunk := decode_chunk_at(cursor)
        next := cursor + chunk.extent
        if chunk.allocated and marked(cursor):
            emit_free_run_into_bins(run)
            run := empty
            clear_mark(cursor)
        else:
            extend_contiguous_run(run, cursor, next)
        cursor := next
    emit_free_run_into_bins(run)
\end{verbatim}

This sketch assumes a single swept region; a multi-region
runtime marks the entire graph before sweeping any region.
Save the next address while the old chunk header is available.
Emitting a free run can overwrite dead headers and install
links. Keeping an iterator into a dead object's payload during
that step is unsafe. Free-list insertion happens once per
maximal run, so no stale nodes from the old bins need to be
unlinked as they are merged. Reset surviving mark bits for
the next collection; epoch bits can remove this particular
write if their reuse policy avoids confusing old and new marks.

\section{Choose Bin and Split Policies Together}

A size-class bin gives quick access to common sizes, but a
class can contain several actual extents. If the class covers
48 through 63 bytes and a request is 56, a 48-byte item
cannot be returned. Pick classes with exact sizes for small
chunks, inspect candidate sizes, or search a class whose
minimum is large enough. If removing a 96-byte chunk leaves
16 bytes after serving a request, the split is legal only if
16 bytes can encode the free chunk. Otherwise the object gets
the whole chunk and the walker needs a way to learn the true
extent rather than recomputing a smaller extent from its payload.
Separate internal fragmentation from external fragmentation:
the first is unused space in allocated chunks, while the second
is free space split into chunks that cannot meet a request.

A collector can report half the heap free while failing a
moderate allocation. For example, alternating live 64-byte
and free 64-byte chunks yield plenty of free bytes but no
128-byte contiguous extent. Coalescing does nothing in this
case; every free chunk already has a live neighbor. Possible
responses include a distinct large-object region, expansion,
or a moving collection such as \Cref{ch:ext-414}. Measure largest free extent alongside
total free bytes. An adaptive collector that reacts only to
free percentage may repeat the same expensive sweep and
obtain the same insufficient result.

\section{Turn the Heap Walk Into a Consistency Check}

After a collection, walk the region without using the bins.
Verify complete, nonoverlapping coverage, aligned starts,
valid descriptors for survivors, and no mergeable adjacent
free extents. Then traverse each bin and compare its nodes to
the free extents discovered independently. A free chunk absent
from the bins silently leaks capacity; the same chunk appearing
twice can be returned to two different clients. Use a sequence
that fills every size class, frees alternating objects, and later
removes an intervening survivor. The first sweep must leave
holes; the second must merge across the newly dead chunk.

Finally, distinguish the collector's search cost from its sweep
cost. Marking visits reachable objects and their fields. A
linear sweep visits each physical chunk, live or dead; it need
not touch every byte of each one. The choice of side bitmaps,
header marks, or lazy per-page sweeping will affect memory
traffic and latency even when the high-level algorithm is the
same. Check pause distribution and the allocation slow path:
delaying a sweep until a bin runs out simply transfers work to
that particular allocation. The caller's observed latency remains
part of the memory manager's behavior.

\section{Give Large Objects Their Own Accounting}

A contiguous page span can use a page descriptor to record
extent and allocation state rather than adding a list node for
every page. Coalesce only spans in the same address range and
allocator domain. A free span that has been returned to the
operating system is not immediately equivalent to mapped,
committed storage; committing pages can be work on the next
allocation. Count committed bytes and reserved address space
separately, and record which component holds the metadata for a
span after decommit. A heap checker must not fault merely to
verify that an uncommitted span is listed once.

Collecting while bins are empty is an important failure test.
Cap the mark stack well below the maximum work for a wide live
graph, then either reject the collection without sweeping or
exercise the specified fallback. One fallback scans marked
objects for unmarked children until no discovered edge adds
a mark, discarding no live work. Its cost can be high on a
deep graph with an unfavorable physical order; count repeated
passes. Also try the opposite shape, a long singly linked
chain, which often uses little explicit worklist space.
The two fixtures distinguish graph depth from frontier size.
Log the fallback separately so routine pause summaries cannot
hide a repeated failure to reserve enough work memory.
